---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CapturePlaceholder from '../../components/CapturePlaceholder.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import StatusChip from '../../components/StatusChip.astro';

const home = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="One plan, many futures | Seculana"
  description="See how Seculana models uncertainty with stable random draws, Monte Carlo explorations, reproducible seeds, and reports of distributions, paths, shortfall chances, and correlations."
>
  <SiteHeader />

  <main id="main" class="deep-page">
    <section class="deep-hero uncertainty-hero section-shell">
      <a class="back-link" href={`${home}showcase/decisions/`}>← Back to decisions</a>
      <p class="eyebrow">Uncertainty without losing the model</p>
      <h1>One plan. Many futures.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The deterministic plan still matters. In an ordinary run, random assumptions use their
        ordinary value. Ask for Monte Carlo runs and those same declarations draw reproducible
        futures instead.
      </p>

      <div class="certainty-switch" aria-label="How a random value behaves in ordinary and Monte Carlo runs">
        <div>
          <span>Ordinary run</span>
          <strong>Returns = 5%</strong>
          <small>the distribution's mean</small>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <div>
          <span>Monte Carlo</span>
          <strong>Returns = a draw</strong>
          <small>same seed + same run → same draw</small>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section story-section--ink">
      <div class="section-shell deep-split">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--light">1 · Put uncertainty where it belongs</p>
          <h2>The assumption moves. The financial semantics do not.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            Lise and Omar's base plan assumes 5% investment returns. The Markets scenario replaces
            that one assumption with a normal draw whose mean is still 5%, with an 11% standard deviation.
          </p>
          <StatusChip tone="live" label="Implemented" />
        </div>
        <div class="code-panel">
          <p class="code-panel-label">examples/lise-and-omar.sec</p>
          <pre><code><span class="code-key">scenario</span> Markets &#123;
    let Returns: series&lt;rate&gt; = normal(5%, 11%)
&#125;</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-shell distribution-deck" aria-label="Selected random mechanisms available in Seculana">
        <div><strong>normal</strong><span>mean + spread</span></div>
        <div><strong>lognormal</strong><span>positive, skewed</span></div>
        <div><strong>triangular / PERT</strong><span>low · likely · high</span></div>
        <div><strong>chance</strong><span>true / false events</span></div>
        <div><strong>sample</strong><span>draw from a table</span></div>
        <div><strong>correlate</strong><span>move values together</span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section story-section--proof">
      <div class="section-shell deep-split">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">2 · Repeat the whole plan</p>
          <h2>A run is a future, not a detached formula.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            Each future still contains pensions, taxes, minimum withdrawals, draws, balances,
            shortfalls, and every other rule in the model. Only the assumptions that draw are different.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="code-panel code-panel--light">
          <p class="code-panel-label">500 reproducible futures</p>
          <pre><code><span class="code-key">explore</span> Risk &#123;
    <span class="code-key">scenario</span> Markets
    <span class="code-key">runs</span> 500
    <span class="code-key">seed</span> 7
    <span class="code-key">show</span> EstateToday, TotalTax
&#125;</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-shell run-grid" aria-label="Conceptual Monte Carlo runs">
        <div><span>run 1</span><i style="--p: 44%"></i><small>same model</small></div>
        <div><span>run 2</span><i style="--p: 67%"></i><small>different draws</small></div>
        <div><span>run 3</span><i style="--p: 31%"></i><small>same seed reproduces it</small></div>
        <div><span>…</span><i style="--p: 55%"></i><small>500 futures</small></div>
      </div>
      <p class="section-shell fine-print">
        The bars are conceptual run identities, not Seculana output values.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section">
      <div class="section-shell">
        <div class="section-heading section-heading--wide">
          <p class="eyebrow">3 · Summarize the spread</p>
          <h2>The answer becomes a distribution.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            A single ending estate is no longer enough. Reports can show the distribution itself,
            how the spread changes over time, representative paths, the chance of shortfall, and
            which outcomes move together.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="chance-kinds">
          <article><span>Histogram</span><strong>Where do outcomes land?</strong><small>median marked</small></article>
          <article><span>Box</span><strong>How wide is each year's spread?</strong><small>5th through 95th</small></article>
          <article><span>Spaghetti</span><strong>What do whole paths look like?</strong><small>50 representative runs</small></article>
          <article><span>Chance</span><strong>How often does a condition happen?</strong><small>including ruin by then</small></article>
          <article><span>Correlation</span><strong>What moves together?</strong><small>rank correlation</small></article>
          <article><span>Coverage</span><strong>What pays the need?</strong><small>shortfall stays visible</small></article>
        </div>

        <div class="fan-concept" aria-label="Conceptual percentile fan">
          <div class="fan-axis"><span>early</span><span>late</span></div>
          <div class="fan-layer fan-layer--95"></div>
          <div class="fan-layer fan-layer--75"></div>
          <div class="fan-layer fan-layer--50"></div>
          <div class="fan-line"></div>
        </div>
        <p class="fine-print">
          Conceptual percentile fan only. The authentic charts below come from Seculana's report renderer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section story-section--showcase">
      <div class="section-shell uncertainty-proof">
        <div class="uncertainty-proof__copy">
          <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--light">4 · Let Seculana draw it</p>
          <h2>A self-contained report turns the runs into evidence.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            The Chance report reuses the same 500 runs across its charts. The figures are worked out
            by Seculana; the page is the renderer, not a second calculation engine.
          </p>

          <div class="code-panel">
            <p class="code-panel-label">report Chance</p>
            <pre><code><span class="code-key">report</span> Chance &#123;
    chart histogram EstateToday over Risk
    chart box Savings over Risk from 2030 to 2055
    chart spaghetti Savings over Risk
    chart chance Short over Risk
    chart correlation EstateToday, TotalTax, YearsShort over Risk
    chart coverage Spending by IncomeAfterTax, Living.Paid
&#125;</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <CapturePlaceholder
            id="CAP-004"
            alt="Lise and Omar uncertainty report showing the distribution of estate outcomes across 500 market futures."
            aspect="standard"
          />
          <p class="capture-caption capture-caption--light">
            Authentic capture pending. CAP-004 asks for the real EstateToday histogram over 500 runs.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section story-section--roadmap">
      <div class="section-shell deep-split">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">5 · Reproducible chance</p>
          <h2>Random does not have to mean untraceable.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            Draws are keyed to the declaration and call that owns them. Repeat the same exploration
            with the same seed and run number and the draw repeats. Editing unrelated code does not
            shuffle the sample underneath you.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="repro-card">
          <span>seed 7 · run 143 · Returns#1</span>
          <strong>same identity</strong>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <strong>same draw</strong>
          <small>until the thing that owns the draw is renamed or changed</small>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section closing-section">
      <div class="section-shell closing-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">The distinction</p>
          <h2>Uncertainty is not prophecy.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="closing-copy">
          <p>
            Monte Carlo does not tell you what will happen. It tells you what this financial system
            does across the futures implied by the assumptions you wrote.
          </p>
          <p>
            That keeps the question inspectable: change the distribution, correlation, policy, or
            constraint and run the same model again.
          </p>
          <a class="button button--solid" href={`${home}#proof`}>Back to the main story</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer section-shell">
    <p><strong>Seculana</strong> · One plan, many futures.</p>
    <p>Conceptual graphics are labelled. Product proof remains authentic Seculana output.</p>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>
